142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Whenever computational teambuilding comes up I always urge that the most important thing is to scope the problem. You mentioned wanting to build a tool "to help people with competitive teambuilding"-- if by this you meant "something that can create a team for me straight up", I do have to say as a battler that plays often at Worlds/etc, I think that the state of AI teambuilding is truthfully very hard from anything that could be useful to me as a competitor. \(If I thought we were close, I would have built these tools!\). Trying to use a program to get a teambuilding advantage vs a slate of expert-level players is very challenging when there is little scientific/computational/research foundation to build on top of. I think that building a system like that end-to-end for an entire format would be very challenging to evaluate. Notice some nice things about the little cup genetic algorithm that made it easier for me to give it a go \(even though the team sucked\): metagames with zero development or knowledge, a closed pool of 8 players i might face, and, most importantly, for-fun evaluation. That's very different than building for the World Championships, or even for the ladder.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Part of the reason that it is difficult to evaluate the strength of computational teambuilding AI is-- what is the evaluation criteria, really? For players or for the agents? I actually called my genetic algorithm for the little cup team the "signal from noise" machine. For that project specifically, my goal was to hardcode as many heuristics as I could given my own preferences, and see whether that got to anywhere interesting. But ultimately I was going to be the one to interpret that and to derive some meaning from that. I wonder, given that same team, what the reaction would have been like if I won a set? What if I won all of my sets? There isn't really a way to tell what makes a team good, and so things get kind of hard if you don't define that for yourself IMO. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
